From c7f6dabfeca575fdbf5d48c3a80044bed7571ad5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:12:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fat: set the modification date when a file is written

The write date of a directory entry was set when the entry was created,
kept by a rename and changed by ACTION_SET_DATE, but never when the
file's contents changed. Overwriting a file in place, replacing it with
MODE_NEWFILE, appending to it or changing its length with SetFileSize()
all left the date the file had before. Programs that look for changed
files by size and date, such as backup and synchronisation tools, do not
see an edit that keeps the size, and the FAT specification has the write
time updated whenever a file is written.

OpWrite(), the truncation of an existing file for ACTION_FINDOUTPUT and
OpSetFileSize() now store the current time in the entry, as SFS does.
OpWrite() only rewrites the entry for the date when the date in FAT's
two-second resolution differs from the one it last stored through the
same lock, so a run of writes costs at most one extra entry update per
two seconds. A date set with SetFileDate() stays until the contents
change again, also when it is set while the file is still open.

The stored date is kept at the end of struct ExtFileLock. Keeping it in
struct GlobalLock would change the layout of struct VolumeInfo, which
embeds one; a handler that adopts a volume left by another handler
instance (DoDiskInsert) then reads that instance's VolumeInfo with the
wrong offsets and crashes, as it did in the test below when the new
handler was mounted after an unchanged one.

New test developer/debug/test/filesys/fat/fatwritedate writes an empty
FAT32 image to RAM:, mounts it through fdsk.device, gives each test file
a date three days in the past, changes it in one way and compares the
date Examine() reports with the time of the change, before and after
mounting the volume again. A date still three days old means it was not
updated; a date within a few seconds of the change counts as current, so
FAT's two-second steps do not affect the result. Opening a file for
writing without writing, and SetFileDate() after a write, must keep the
date that was set. HANDLER <path> mounts the image with a handler file
instead of the system's one.
